          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Fire Bowl, Fire Pit Table or Tabletop: Which Gas Fire Pit Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best gas fire pit depends first on its shape, because shape decides where it can go, how many people it seats and how much of the tank you can hide. Three styles cover almost every buyer, and each suits a different patio and a different evening.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Portable Fire Bowls</h3>

          <p>
            Fire bowls like the Outland Firebowl 870 are 19 to 24 inches across, sit low on short legs and connect to a 20 lb tank on a long hose. They are light, need no assembly and travel to campsites and tailgates. They suit two to four people and put out a surprising amount of heat for their size, but they offer no tabletop and the tank sits on the ground next to them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fire Pit Tables</h3>

          <p>
            Fire pit tables like the Real Flame Aegean, Outland Series 403 and Endless Summer Lindsey put the burner in the middle of a coffee-height table. The ledge holds drinks, a lid or insert turns them into a table when the fire is off, and some hide the tank inside. They are the most popular choice for a permanent patio setting, and the only style that suits a natural gas line.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tabletop and Combination Units</h3>

          <p>
            Small tabletop burners give a candle-sized flame for ambience, not warmth. At the other end, combination units like the Endless Summer DualHeat Donovan add a patio heater element below the fire to warm legs and feet, which a plain fire table rarely does. If you mainly want heat on cold nights, a combination unit is worth a look before a larger flame.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Wood Still Wins</h3>

          <p>
            If you want crackling logs, cooking over coals and the smell of a real fire, a gas pit will disappoint. Our <a href="/best-fire-pit/">best fire pit guide</a> covers smokeless wood-burning pits from Solo Stove and Breeo, plus a simple propane bowl for comparison. Choose gas for instant, clean, controllable flame; choose wood for the campfire experience.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="btu-burn-time" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. BTU and Burn Time: How Long a 20 lb Tank Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            BTU is the number every gas fire pit listing leads with, and it tells you two things: how much heat the burner can put out at full flame and how fast it empties your tank. The models in this guide range from 41,000 to 72,000 BTU.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the BTU Rating Means</h3>

          <p>
            The rating is the maximum heat output per hour with the valve fully open. A 50,000 BTU table is the common standard for patio fire tables, and 58,000 BTU from a small Outland bowl is a lot of heat concentrated in a small area. You will often run the flame below its maximum, which stretches each tank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Burn-Time Arithmetic</h3>

          <p>
            A full 20 lb propane tank holds roughly 4.7 gallons, or about 430,000 BTU of energy. Divide that by the burner's rating to estimate full-flame burn time: around 8.5 hours at 50,000 BTU, around 7.4 hours at 58,000 BTU, and around 6 hours at 72,000 BTU. Solo Stove lists up to 5.5 hours for the Infinity Flame, which matches that math. These are rough estimates; cold weather, wind and partly full tanks all cut them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan on a Spare Tank</h3>

          <p>
            For regular use, keep a second filled tank so the fire never dies mid-evening. Store spare tanks upright, outdoors and away from the fire pit, never in a garage, basement or car. If you burn several nights a week, the fuel bill is the point where a natural gas line starts to make sense.
          </p>

