        <div id="propane-vs-natural-gas" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Propane vs Natural Gas Fire Pits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many buyers want a natural gas fire pit, but most fire pits on Amazon ship set up for propane. Here is how the two compare and how conversion works.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Propane: Flexible and Portable</h3>

          <p>
            Propane runs from a standard 20 lb grill tank, so a propane fire pit can go anywhere on the patio, move with the seasons and travel. Setup is minutes, and there is no installation. The downsides are swapping tanks, storing them safely and a higher cost per hour of fire than natural gas in most areas.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Natural Gas: Endless Fuel, Fixed Location</h3>

          <p>
            A natural gas fire pit connects to your home's gas supply, so it never runs out and costs less per hour to run. It is fixed in place, though, and needs a gas line run to the patio. That work means a permit in many areas and, in every case, a licensed gas installer or plumber. Never run your own gas line or connect a fire pit to it yourself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Convertible Models</h3>

          <p>
            The Real Flame Aegean ships with a natural gas conversion kit and says the conversion must be done by a certified gas technician. Outland sells optional CSA-approved conversion kits for its Firebowls, such as kit 785 for the 870. Only use the conversion kit the maker specifies for your exact model; propane and natural gas burn at different pressures and use different orifices, so mixing parts is dangerous.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which to Choose</h3>

          <p>
            If you already have a gas line near the patio or plan to use the fire pit most evenings, natural gas wins. If you rent, may move it, or use it a few times a month, propane is simpler. A convertible table lets you start on propane and switch later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lava-rock-vs-glass" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Lava Rock vs Fire Glass: Does the Filler Matter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas fire pits need a filler over the burner. It spreads the gas so the flame looks natural, hides the burner and holds some heat. The two common fillers look very different.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lava Rock</h3>

          <p>
            Lava rock is porous volcanic stone with a natural, campfire-like look. The Real Flame Aegean, Outland Firebowl 870 and Amazon Basics table all come with it. It is inexpensive and forgiving, but it can trap rainwater; damp lava rock can pop or crack when heated, so keep the fire pit covered and dry it with a short, low burn after rain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fire Glass</h3>

          <p>
            Tempered fire glass reflects the flame for a modern, sparkling look. The Outland Series 403, Endless Summer Lindsey, DualHeat Donovan and Solo Stove Infinity Flame use glass. It does not absorb water the way lava rock does, but it shows soot if the flame burns dirty, and it costs more to top up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Adding or Swapping Filler</h3>

          <p>
            Use only filler rated for gas fire pits; ordinary river rock or gravel can trap moisture and crack violently when heated. Keep the depth the manual recommends so the burner ports are covered but not buried, and never pile filler over the pilot or ignition.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Gas Fire Pit Safety: The Rules That Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            A gas fire pit is safer than a wood fire in many ways, with no sparks or embers, but it is still an open flame fed by pressurized fuel. These rules apply to every model here; your manual's rules always come first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Outdoors Only</h3>

          <p>
            Every gas fire pit in this guide is for outdoor use only. Burning propane or natural gas produces carbon monoxide, which builds up in enclosed or partly enclosed spaces. Never use one in a garage, tent, sunroom or screened porch. Keeping a <a href="/best-carbon-monoxide-detector/">carbon monoxide detector</a> inside near doors to the patio is a sensible backup.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep the Listed Clearances</h3>

          <p>
            Makers list minimum clearances to overhead and side combustibles. The Outland Series 403, for example, lists 80 inches above and 36 inches to the sides. Measure from roofs, branches, umbrellas, railings, siding and furniture, and follow the figures in your own manual, not a general rule.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Under a Covered Patio Unless the Maker Allows It</h3>

          <p>
            Pergolas, awnings and covered patios trap heat and gas. Use a fire pit under any cover only if the manual specifically allows it and the required overhead clearance and ventilation are met. If it does not say so, assume it is not allowed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Leak-Test Every Connection</h3>

          <p>
            Before first use and every time you connect a tank, brush soapy water over the hose, regulator and tank valve with the gas on and the burner off. Bubbles mean a leak: turn the gas off, tighten or replace the part, and test again. If you smell gas, shut off the supply and do not light the fire.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Follow Local Codes and Burn Rules</h3>

          <p>
            Fire codes, HOA rules, apartment leases and burn bans can all restrict fire pits, and gas pits are not automatically exempt. Check before you buy. Some campgrounds allow CSA-approved propane fire pits during wood-fire bans, as Outland notes, but always check the current restrictions where you are.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="decks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Can You Use a Gas Fire Pit on a Wood or Composite Deck?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas fire pits are the usual choice for decks because they throw no sparks or embers, but not every model is approved for one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Listing and Manual</h3>

          <p>
            The Real Flame Aegean states it is safe for use on a wooden deck. The Outland Series 403 says to install it on or over non-combustible floors only, and Endless Summer recommends non-combustible floors for the Donovan. Those instructions are the deciding factor, so read them before you put any fire pit on a deck.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Protect the Surface</h3>

          <p>
            Even a deck-rated table heats the boards below over a long evening. A fire-resistant deck pad or a non-combustible base reduces the risk of scorching, discoloration and, on composite decking, warping. Keep the tank and hose where nobody will trip on them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Composite Decking Is Not Fireproof</h3>

          <p>
            Composite boards can soften, warp and burn. Many composite makers publish their own rules for fire pits, so check your decking maker's guidance as well as the fire pit's.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Size Gas Fire Pit Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Size the fire pit to the number of people and the space you have, then check clearances before you order.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two to Four People</h3>

          <p>
            A 19- to 24-inch bowl like the Outland 870, or a 28- to 30-inch table like the Lindsey or Amazon Basics, suits a couple or a small family. A smaller pit means everyone sits closer to the heat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Five to Eight People</h3>

          <p>
            Rectangular tables of 42 to 44 inches, like the Real Flame Aegean, Outland Series 403 and Solo Stove Infinity Flame, seat more people and leave room for drinks. Solo Stove lists five to eight for the Infinity Flame.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Leave Room Around It</h3>

          <p>
            Allow walking space around the pit and seats at a comfortable distance, typically a few feet from the edge, plus the maker's side and overhead clearances to anything that can burn.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Are Gas Fire Pits Warm? Getting Real Heat
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas fire pits are known for ambience more than heat, and many owners are surprised how little warmth reaches them. A few things make a real difference.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat Rises</h3>

          <p>
            A gas flame sends most of its heat upward. Sitting closer helps, as does a lower profile: Outland says the lower profile of its 21-inch Cypress bowl increases the functional heat radius. Wind steals heat too, so a wind guard or a sheltered spot, within the allowed clearances, helps more than turning the flame up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose a Heater Combination</h3>

          <p>
            The Endless Summer DualHeat Donovan adds an 11,000 BTU heater below the mantel aimed at legs and feet. For cold nights, that warms people more effectively than a bigger flame alone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use the Right Tool</h3>

          <p>
            If heat matters more than flame, a patio heater may suit you better. Never bring an outdoor gas fire pit indoors for warmth; for indoor heat, use a heater rated for indoor use, like those in our <a href="/best-indoor-propane-heater/">indoor propane heater guide</a>, and follow its ventilation rules.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Set Up and Light a Gas Fire Pit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Setup is simple, but the first lighting is where most mistakes happen. Follow the manual and these steps.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Place It on a Level, Allowed Surface</h3>

          <p>
            Set the fire pit on a stable, level surface the maker allows, with the listed clearances on all sides and above. Use adjustable feet, like those on the Lindsey and Aegean, to level it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Connect and Leak-Test</h3>

          <p>
            Make sure the burner valve is off, connect the regulator to the tank hand-tight, open the tank valve slowly and leak-test with soapy water.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Add Filler Correctly</h3>

          <p>
            Spread the lava rock or glass to the depth the manual specifies, keeping the ignition and pilot area clear.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Light at Low Flame</h3>

          <p>
            Light with the push-button or auto-ignition at the lowest setting, then turn the flame up. If it does not light within a few seconds, turn the gas off and wait for the gas to clear before trying again.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Shut Down Properly</h3>

          <p>
            Turn off the burner and then the tank valve, let the fire pit cool completely, and only then fit the lid or cover.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Covers, Cleaning and Winter Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            A gas fire pit lasts longer and lights more reliably with simple care.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cover It Between Uses</h3>

          <p>
            Rain and debris clog burner ports and soak lava rock. Most picks here include a cover; use it once the unit is cool. Real Flame says to make sure the unit has cooled completely before using the burner lid or storage cover.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep the Burner Clear</h3>

          <p>
            Spiders and insects like to nest in gas lines and burners, which causes weak or uneven flames. Inspect the burner and ports at the start of each season, and inspect the hose and regulator for cracks or wear, replacing parts as the maker recommends.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Winter Storage</h3>

          <p>
            In cold climates, disconnect the tank and store it upright outdoors, away from the house. Store the fire pit covered or indoors if the manual allows, and leak-test again before the first fire of spring.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="certification" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. CSA Certification and What the Listings Tell You
          </h2>

          <p>
            Certification marks show a fire pit has been evaluated against a recognized safety standard. Several picks here state one, and some do not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Picks List a Certification</h3>

          <p>
            Real Flame lists the Aegean as CSA Certified, and Outland lists the Firebowl 870 as CSA approved. The Solo Stove, Endless Summer and Amazon Basics listings we read do not state a certification, which does not mean they lack one, only that the listing does not say. Check the product label, manual or maker's site if certification matters to you, for instance to meet campground rules.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Safety Features to Look For</h3>

          <p>
            Look for an automatic safety shut-off, which stops gas flow if the flame goes out. The Aegean, Donovan and Lindsey list one. Hidden control panels, like those on the Endless Summer tables, keep knobs away from curious children.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Gas Fire Pit in an Enclosed Space</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Garages, sunrooms, screened porches and tents trap carbon monoxide. Every fire pit here is for outdoor use only, in a well-ventilated area.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Listed Clearances</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Overhead and side clearances are in the manual for a reason. Measure to roofs, umbrellas, branches and siding before you place the fire pit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Soapy-Water Leak Test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A loose regulator connection can leak gas silently. Leak-test every time you connect a tank, and never light it if you smell gas.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Natural Gas Line Yourself</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gas line work needs a licensed installer and often a permit. Conversion kits must also be fitted as the maker specifies, often by a certified technician.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a Non-Deck-Rated Table on a Deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some tables, like the Outland Series 403, are for non-combustible floors only. Check the listing and manual before using any fire pit on wood or composite decking.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

