        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best gas fire pit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most patios, the Real Flame Aegean 42-inch is the best gas fire pit. Its listing includes both a propane hose and a natural gas conversion kit, a 50,000 BTU burner, an automatic shut-off, CSA certification and approval for wooden decks. For a portable model, the Outland Firebowl 870 Premium is the best choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best outdoor natural gas fire pit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Real Flame Aegean is the best natural gas option we found on Amazon because the conversion kit and a 10-foot NG hose come in the box. Outland Firebowls take an optional natural gas kit. Any natural gas hookup must be installed by a licensed gas professional, and many areas require a permit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a 20 lb propane tank last in a fire pit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roughly 8.5 hours at full flame on a 50,000 BTU burner, about 7.4 hours at 58,000 BTU and about 6 hours at 72,000 BTU. Solo Stove lists up to 5.5 hours for the Infinity Flame. Running the flame lower stretches a tank, while cold and wind shorten it, so keep a spare filled tank on hand.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are gas fire pits worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you want an instant, smoke-free flame with no ash, sparks or firewood to store. Gas pits work on many decks and patios where wood fires are not allowed. They give less heat and less campfire character than wood, so choose wood if you want to cook over coals.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a gas fire pit under a covered patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the maker's manual specifically allows it and the listed overhead clearance and ventilation are met. Covers trap heat and combustion gases. The Outland Series 403, for example, lists 80 inches of clearance above. If the manual does not allow covered use, do not do it, and never use one in a screened or enclosed porch.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is lava rock or fire glass better for a gas fire pit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither burns better; the choice is mostly looks. Lava rock is cheaper and looks like a campfire, but it soaks up rain and should be dried before a full flame. Fire glass reflects the flame for a modern look and does not soak up water. Use only filler made for gas fire pits.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do gas fire pits give off heat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but most of it rises. A 50,000 BTU table warms people seated nearby on a mild evening, and less in wind or deep cold. For real warmth, sit close, shelter it from wind within the allowed clearances, or choose a combination unit like the Endless Summer DualHeat Donovan, which adds a heater aimed at legs.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

